     The structure is a paper (IMRAD), not a walk through the figures: Abstract, Introduction, Methods, Results, Discussion, Conclusion. There is no manual References heading — Quarto appends the bibliography from the `@key` citations and the `bibliography:` set in _quarto.yml. Write mathematics as LaTeX; xelatex drops Unicode. Hold the prose to the writing-reports anti-slop standard before shipping: at most one em-dash per paragraph, no "robust/comprehensive/seamless/leverage/furthermore/moreover/in conclusion" filler, no rule-of-three lists or stacked adjectives, active voice, one idea per sentence. -->

# Abstract {.unnumbered}

<One paragraph, and the first place a citation appears: introduce the paper as `@<Author><Year>`, an inline `@key` from references.bib that Quarto resolves and lists once at the end, so you never write a References section. The paper's core result; that the whole study is a single declarative recipe (`<Study>.yaml`) whose equations are rendered and run from the same metadata; the headline that reproduces; and, stated honestly up front, the one thing that does not. Every number inline-computed from M, none typed by hand.>

# Introduction

<Motivate: the paper's question and contribution (`@<Author><Year>`), why replicate it in TVBO (declarative, native equations, backend-independent), and the scope — which figures and targets. Preview the reproduction-versus-replication framing and any negative result: establishing one rigorously is part of an honest replication, not something to hide. Cite background work inline with `@key`; a bare `@key` renders "Author (Year)", a bracketed `[@key]` renders "(Author, Year)".>

# Methods

## Model and equations (rendered natively from the recipe)

The model is **generated from the recipe** — the equations and parameters shown are exactly what runs on the backend, not a hand-transcribed copy, so they can be checked one to one against the paper's equations. `citeformat="quarto"` makes the render emit the model's own references as inline `@key` citations, resolved by this document's bibliography and merged into the one auto-appended list, which is why the model's citekeys must exist in `references.bib`.

```{python}
